(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["pharmacology-i-exam-3-asthma-bronchodilators-quiz"]={"title":"Asthma & the Bronchodilators Quiz 1 — Pharmacology I Exam 3","category":"Pharmacology I","exam":"Exam 3","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"Bronchial reactivity is assessed by the fall in forced expiratory volume in 1 second after a patient inhales increasing concentrations of which agent?","choices":["Albuterol","Ipratropium","Cromolyn","Methacholine"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Bronchial reactivity is assessed by measuring the fall in forced expiratory volume in 1 second after inhaling increasing concentrations of methacholine. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 62"},{"q":"Albuterol, ipratropium and theophylline share which therapeutic approach to asthma?","choices":["Removing the trigger","Reversing bronchospasm","Controlling inflammation","Blocking immunoglobulin E"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Beta-2 agonists, ipratropium and theophylline all reverse bronchospasm; cromolyn and nedocromil remove the trigger, and steroids control inflammation. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 64"},{"q":"Which long-term controller medication is a methylxanthine?","choices":["Theophylline","Montelukast","Cromolyn","Salmeterol"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Theophylline is the methylxanthine among the long-term controllers; it is a nonspecific phosphodiesterase inhibitor with a narrow therapeutic index. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 67"},{"q":"Which drug is the intravenous form of theophylline?","choices":["Aminophylline","Methylprednisolone","Reslizumab","Levalbuterol"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Theophylline is the methylxanthine, and aminophylline is its intravenous form. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 82"},{"q":"How does theophylline compare with long-acting bronchodilators?","choices":["More effective but less well tolerated","Less effective and less well tolerated","Equally effective and better tolerated","Less effective but better tolerated"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Compared with long-acting bronchodilators, methylxanthines are less effective and less well tolerated, and theophylline has limited use. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 82"},{"q":"Besides convulsions, which danger of high theophylline levels comes from its action as a cardiac stimulant?","choices":["Bradycardia","Hypotension","Arrhythmias","Pulmonary edema"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Theophylline is a cardiac stimulant, and high levels cause arrhythmias; as a central nervous system stimulant it also causes convulsions. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 84"},{"q":"Which eye finding is part of anticholinergic toxicity?","choices":["Miosis","Red-green color loss","Mydriasis","Orange-colored tears"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. 'Blind as a bat' stands for blurry vision and mydriasis (dilated pupils) in anticholinergic toxicity. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 87"},{"q":"Which inhaled bronchodilator is a short-acting beta-2 agonist?","choices":["Salmeterol","Formoterol","Tiotropium","Levalbuterol"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The short-acting beta-2 agonists are albuterol (Proair, Ventolin) and levalbuterol (Xopenex). — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 70"},{"q":"Which skin finding is part of anticholinergic toxicity?","choices":["Urticaria","Dermal thinning","Diaphoresis","Flushing"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. 'Red as a beet' stands for flushing in anticholinergic toxicity, along with fever and hyperthermia ('hot as Hades'). — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 87"},{"q":"Which drug works rapidly and lasts about 4 to 6 hours?","choices":["Salmeterol","Tiotropium","Albuterol","Formoterol"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Short-acting beta-2 agonists such as albuterol work rapidly and last 4 to 6 hours, which suits quick relief. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 68"},{"q":"Besides inhibiting phosphodiesterase, which receptor action is part of theophylline's mechanism?","choices":["Muscarinic receptor blockade","Adenosine receptor antagonism","Beta-2 receptor stimulation","Leukotriene receptor blockade"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Theophylline inhibits phosphodiesterase (4 and 5), raising cyclic adenosine and guanosine monophosphate, antagonizes the adenosine A2B receptor, and has anti-inflammatory actions in the late response. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 83"},{"q":"Which restriction applies to long-acting beta-2 agonists in asthma?","choices":["They must not be used alone","They must be taken before exposure","They need plasma level monitoring","They must be given intravenously"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Long-acting beta-2 agonists (formoterol, salmeterol) are not to be used alone for asthma; the combination inhalers pair each with an inhaled corticosteroid. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 70"},{"q":"Which bronchodilator lasts 24 hours or more?","choices":["Tiotropium","Ipratropium","Albuterol","Levalbuterol"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Tiotropium (Spiriva) is the long-acting anticholinergic and lasts 24 hours or more; ipratropium lasts up to 8 hours. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 86"},{"q":"Besides treating bronchospasm with a short-acting beta-2 agonist, what helps prevent acute bronchospasm in asthma?","choices":["Taking routine antibiotics","Exercising in cold air","Inhaling distilled water mist","Avoiding allergens and triggers"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Acute bronchospasm is prevented or reversed by avoiding allergens and asthma triggers and by treating with short-term beta-2 agonists. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 66"},{"q":"Which central nervous system effect is listed in anticholinergic toxicity?","choices":["Delirium","Emergence phenomenon","Euphoria","Insomnia"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. 'Mad as a hatter' stands for hallucinations, delirium, memory loss and psychosis in anticholinergic toxicity. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 87"},{"q":"Which combination of heart rate and blood pressure changes is listed among the adverse effects of beta-2 agonists?","choices":["Tachycardia and hypertension","Bradycardia and hypotension","Bradycardia and hypertension","Tachycardia and hypotension"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The beta-2 agonist adverse effects are tachycardia, hypotension, headache, dizziness, insomnia, tremors and hyperglycemia. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 71"},{"q":"Which type of inflammation of the bronchial mucosa characterizes asthma?","choices":["Neutrophilic","Eosinophilic","Granulomatous","Lymphocytic"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Asthma is widespread, reversible narrowing of the bronchial airways with eosinophilic inflammation of the bronchial mucosa and a marked increase in bronchial responsiveness. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 57"},{"q":"What triggers most asthma attacks?","choices":["Allergen exposure","Cold air exposure","Viral infections","Aspirin ingestion"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Most asthma attacks are not triggered by allergens; most are triggered by viral respiratory infection. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 62"}]};
